prompt: Load the `notes` skill from ~/.claude/skills/notes/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/notes/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[notes] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 5–10 sentences: if I say `add note for: remind me to file my [[taxes]] by Friday`, what exactly will you do? Cover classification, vault selection, the destination file, the file's frontmatter and section shape, and any topic-stub side effects.
expect_substr: [notes] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers five behaviours. (1) Classification — recognises this as a reminder (the "remind me to" phrasing plus the date hint "Friday"); does NOT classify it as a 1:1, conversation, or insight. (2) Vault selection — uses the default chain (`$NOTES_VAULT` → `$HOME/notes`) because no `in <vault>` qualifier is present. (3) Destination file — writes to `reminders/inbox.md` (a rolling file) or a topic-area file like `reminders/<slug>.md`. The filename is slug-only — NOT `reminders/2026-MM-DD-<slug>.md` or any date-prefixed filename. (4) File shape — frontmatter is `kind: reminder` (no top-level `date:` field on a rolling reminders file); a `## YYYY-MM-DD` section header carries today's date; the reminder body is a bullet under that header, with the due date captured inline (e.g. `— due Friday` or `— due 2026-MM-DD`). (5) Topic stub — because `[[taxes]]` is referenced and `topics/taxes.md` doesn't exist, creates a one-line stub with `kind: topic` frontmatter; never overwrites an existing stub. Wrong answers: writing a dated filename like `reminders/2026-05-30-taxes.md`; emitting a top-level `date:` in a rolling reminders file; classifying as insight or 1:1; skipping the topic stub; auto-creating `topics/taxes.md` on top of an existing one.
tools: claude,kiro
